## 1. Introduction
Water is the source of life. Clean water is essential for survival of human and all species around the world. With every passing day of industrial production, a growing number of potable groundwater is in the erosion of industrial wastewater [1–3]. As a common industrial wastewater among these sewages, printing and dyeing wastewater has the characteristics of large water volume, high content of organic pollutants and salts, and large water quality changes, which is one of intractable industrial wastewater. As the main pollutants in printing and dyeing wastewater, dye molecules are the primary substances that need to be removed and filtered in wastewater treatment [4–7]. Currently, a variety of ways are used to treat dyes in printing and dyeing wastewater [8–11]. Membrane separation technology has played an increasingly important role in the field of water treatment [12–14]. The molecular weight (MW) of dye molecules is generally below \(1000\ \mathrm{Da}\), and their removal belongs to the category of nanofiltration in the field of membrane separation [15–17].
As a typical two-dimensional (2D) material, coupled with its good amphiphilic properties, GO can form a stable, light brown-yellow dispersion with water or other solvents, which provides a theoretical basis for the preparation of GO membranes. Many researchers have prepared membranes and realized their application in the field of membrane separation [18–22]. However, there are often many defects in the GO sheet due to its uncertainty functionalized, which greatly affect the separation performance of GO membrane. Therefore, some researchers have embedded small molecules or nanomaterials into the layer spacing of GO by physical incorporation or chemical bonding to control its stability and achieve its precise separation [23–25]. But these small molecule substances occupy positions between the GO sheets, and they often have a strong interaction with GO, that cause the layer spacing to be blocked or narrowed, which seriously affects its water transport capacity [26,27].
To solve the above problems, several researchers often prefer to insert some large size materials into the layer spacing of GO. UiO\(66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) is a kind of porous metal organic frameworks (MOFs) material with stable chemical and physical properties, and there are related works that it had been successfully added into GO membranes [28,29]. However, the interaction force between UiO\(66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) and GO sheet was very weak, which leads to unstable structure and performance of prepared UiO-66-NH2@GO membrane. It have been reported that Zeng et al. modified UiO-66-NH2 with PAA to increase the compatibility between MOFs and GO, and the PAA@UiO/GO composite membrane exhibited excellent performance and stability [30]. PTCDA is a small molecule with polyphenyl ring arrangement similar to GO, which can produce a strong II-II conjugation with GO sheets [31–32]. These provide a novel way for locking UiO-66-NH2 between the layer spacing of GO. In this work, PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2 was inserted the layer spacing of GO to prepare the GO@ PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2 composite membrane. The II-II conjugation between PTCDA and GO provided the binding force of MOFs and GO, narrowing the actual filter channel between the layers, and promoting the removal of dyes. Moreover, the insertion of PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2 created new nodular structures that were conducive to water transport, and PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2 also adsorbed dye molecules laterally benefiting from its large specific surface area and the II-II stacking between PTCDA and dyes, which achieved secondary removal of dyes. This perfectly realized the combination of large size materials and small molecules, and it provided new development prospects for the membrane field.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Graphene oxide powder (GO, \(>98\%\), Suzhou Tanfeng Graphene Technology Co., Ltd.); Deionized (DI)water (Youpu pure water machine); Disperse black 9 (DB9, \(90\%\), K Lamar Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd.); Eriochrome black T (EBT, \(90\%\), Adamas Reagent Co., Ltd.); Polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS, Mw=200 Da, Tianjin Yuyuan Technology Co., Ltd.); Congo red (CR, \(>98.5\%\)) and 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid (BDC, \(>98.5\%\)) were purchased from Tianjin Guangfu Fine Chemical Research Institute; Sodium hydroxide (NaOH, \(>90\%\)) and hydrochloric acid (HCl, \(90\%\)) were purchased from Tianjin Fengchuan Chemical Reagent Technology Co., Ltd.; N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF, \(>99.5\%\)), perylene-3,4,9,10-tetracarboxylic dianhydride (PTCDA, \(98\%\)) and 2-amino-1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid \((2\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-BDC}>98\%)\) were purchased from J&K Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Beijing, China); Absolute ethanol \((>99\%)\), absolute methanol \((>99\%)\) and anhydrous sodium sulfate \((\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4},>99\%)\) were purchased from Kermel Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Tianjin, China); Zirconium chloride \((\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4},\,98\%)\), potassium chloride (KCl, \(99.8\%\)), carboxymethyl cellulose sodium (CMC-Na, viscosity: 300-800mpa.s, USP), Span-80, crystal violet (CV, \(98\%\)), methyl blue (MB, \(>99.7\%\)) and alphazurine A (AZA, \(>99.7\%\)) were purchased from Aladdin Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China); The mixed cellulose (MCE) support membrane \((0.22\ \upmu\mathrm{m},\Phi=50\ \mathrm{mm})\) were purchased from Tianjin Yuyuan Technology Co., Ltd.
## 2.2. Hydrolysis of PTCDA
The water dispersibility of PTCDA was poor, and hydrolyzed PTCDA (HPTCDA) was used as a comparison. \(0.5\ \mathrm{g}\) PTCDA was added to \(100\ \mathrm{mL}\ 5\ \mathrm{w/v}\ \%\) NaOH water solution, stirring magnetically for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\). The uniform dispersion was heated to \(80\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), keeping it under magnetic stirring for \(6\ \mathrm{h}\) to hydrolyze it fully. The acquired solution was washed 3 times in the centrifuge at the speed of \(10{,}000\ \mathrm{r}\ /\ \mathrm{min}\), and each time was \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) to remove excess NaOH. Finally, the obtained solid (HPTCDA) was dried in an oven at \(60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for later use. The experimental reaction process was shown in Figure S1(a).
## 2.3. Preparation of PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2 and HPTCDA-UiO-66-NH2
The preparation of UiO-66-NH2 was the previous work as shown in Figure S2, and its characterization could be found in the relevant literature [16]. \(0.1\ \mathrm{mmol}\) PTCDA (or HPTCDA) and \(0.1\ \mathrm{mmol}\) UiO-66-NH2 were added to \(300\ \mathrm{mL}\) methanol, and the dispersion was ultrasonicated for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\). With the continued magnetic stirring, the solution was heated at \(60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(6\ \mathrm{h}\) [29]. The obtained product was centrifuged 3 times at a rate of \(12{,}000\ \mathrm{r}\ /\ \mathrm{min}\) with methanol, and each time lasted \(15\ \mathrm{min}\) for removing excess PTCDA or HPTCDA. Finally, the obtained solid (PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2 or HPTCDA-UiO-66-NH2) was dried in an oven at \(60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for later use. The experimental reaction process was shown in Figure S1 (b, c).
## 2.4. Preparation of membranes
All membranes were prepared by vacuum filtration. GO powder was dispersed in DI water at a concentration of \(20\ \mathrm{mg/L}\), and the above dispersion was ultrasound for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) (400 W, room temperature) to obtain a clear yellow–brown dispersion liquid. MCE sup
port membrane was fixed on the filter element of the suction filter flask, and \(10\;\mathrm{mL}\) GO dispersion liquid was filtered under the pressure of 0.9 bar. The wet compound membrane was dried at room temperature at least \(24~\mathrm{h}\) for later use. The GO membrane (M1) had been prepared, and the preparation of other composite membranes were also followed the above steps. GO and PTCDA (or HPTCDA, or PTCDA-UiO-66-NH\(_2\), or HPTCDA-UiO-66-NH\(_2\)) was dispersed in DI water with a ratio of 1:1, and the concentration of dispersion was still maintained at \(20\;\mathrm{mg/L}\). Following the above, the obtained membranes were \(\mathrm{GO@PTCDA}\) membrane (M2), \(\mathrm{GO@HPTCDA}\) membrane (M3), \(\mathrm{GO@PTCDA-UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) membrane (M4) and \(\mathrm{GO@}\) HPTCDA-UiO-66-NH\(_2\) membrane (M5), respectively. The specific process was shown in Fig. 1. The \(\mathrm{GO@}\) UiO-66-NH\(_2\) membrane was also prepared as a control group following the above steps, and its performance test was mainly presented in the support information.
