## 2.6. Filter measurements
The filtration performance of the membrane was tested by a dead end filtration system with a stain steel round membrane cell \((\mathsf{e}{-}30\;\mathrm{mm})\) as shown in Figure S3. The membrane was firmly fixed on the surface of the filter element with screws, and the membrane cell was put a pressure of 1 bar with a pressure pump to make the filtrate circulate in the entire device. The feed solutions were all aqueous solutions, including dye solutions, the mixed solutions of dye and salt, and the solutions of organic molecules. The concentration of the dye solution was \(100\;\mathrm{mg/L}\), and the dye was the main dye components in the printing and dyeing wastewater, including: CR, MB, AZA, DB9, EBT and CV. The salt in the mixed solution was \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\). They were all configured by magnetic stirring at room temperature. CMC-Na, Span-80, PDMS and BDC were selected as organic pollutants, and the concentration of the solution was \(100\;\mathrm{mg/L}\). The CMC-Na solution was prepared at \(100\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with long-term magnetic stirring, and Span-80, PDMS and BDC solution needed to be sonicated and stirred alternately at room temperature.
ture for a long time. The entire device circulated at \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and the feed was continuously magnetically stirred. The membrane was pre-pressed for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) to stabilize the entire filter device before each test. The final test result was the average value of 3 times. In the circulation experiment, \(20\ \mathrm{mL}\) dye solution was filtered as one cycle, and \(20\ \mathrm{mL}\) of pure water was used to wash out the membrane. The equation for calculating membrane permeance (PWP) was (1):
\[
\mathrm{PWP}=\mathsf{V}/(\mathsf{A}\times\mathsf{t}\times\mathsf{p})
\]
In which, \(\mathsf{V}\) represented the volume of permeate, A represented the effective area of the membrane cell, t represented the permeation time, and \(\mathbf{p}\) was the pressure during testing. The equation for rejection (R) was (2):
\[
\mathrm{R}(\%)=100\times\left(1-\left(\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{p}}/\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{f}}\right)\right)
\]
In which, \(\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{p}}\) represented the UV absorbance (or total carbon content) of the permeance, and \(\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{f}}\) represented the UV absorbance (or total carbon content) of the feed solution [17].
## 2.7. Adsorption experiment
CV, DB9, CR aqueous solutions were typical pollutants in adsorption experiments, and their UV absorbance was measured as the adsorption data. The M4 were immersed in \(100\ \mathrm{mL}\) \(20\ \mathrm{mg/L}\) of dye wastewater, and the test solution was taken every \(15\ \mathrm{min}\), recording the UV absorbance. Each experiment was carried out 3 times, and the test data were averaged of the 3 times.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Structure and morphology of GO
GO is a 2D material with layer spacing because of being oxidized, and the size of layer spacing mainly depends on the degree of oxidation. Generally, the sufficient oxidation results in wide layer spacing [33,34]. Fig. 2 (a, b) performed XPS results of GO, and its peak area of \(\mathsf{C{-}O{-}C}\) was close to \(\mathsf{C{-}C}\), which indicated that GO had a high degree of oxidation, that leaded to a large d-spacing close to \(1\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Table 1). The surface morphology and thickness of GO sheets can be obtained by TEM and AFM. As shown in Fig. 2 (c, d) and S4, the diameter of GO sheet was about \(1\sim3\ \upmu\mathrm{~m~}\), and its thickness was about \(0.77\ \mathrm{nm}\), proving it was monolithic layer structure [23].
## 3.2. Characterization of PTCDA and PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2
The characterization of \(\mathsf{U i O-66-N H}_{2}\) referred to the previous work [16]. The internal structure of PTCDA was characterized by its XPS survey spectra and FTIR spectra. In Fig. 3 (a), The XPS survey spectra of PTCDA mainly included C peak and O peak, which corresponded to its polyphenyl ring and acid anhydride structure. In Fig. 3 (b), the C1s peak splitting results of PTCDA was consistent with its structure image in Figure S1 (a), and its FTIR spectra also showed the same result in Fig. 3 (e). PTCDA showed multiple inplane and out-of-plane \(\mathsf{C{-}H}\) bending vibration peaks in the \(700-\) \(1250\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) band. The tensile vibration peaks of \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{C}\) and \(\mathsf{C{-}H}\) in the benzene ring appeared at \(1590\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(3110\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), while the characteristic peaks of acid anhydride appeared at \(1295\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1750\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), corresponding to its \(\mathsf{C{-}O{-}C}\) and \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{O}\) stretching vibration, respectively. All these proved that the substance was PTCDA [35,36]. The XPS survey spectra and N1s peak splitting results of PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2 were showed in Fig. 3 (c, d). In Fig. 3 (c), the presence of Zr and N elements proved the successful synthesis of PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2. The content of amide bond could corroborate the yield of PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2, so the N1s peak was separated in Fig. 3 (d). The peak areas of \(\mathsf{-N H}_{2}\) and \(\scriptstyle0=\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{N H}\) were used to estimate their content. And total amount of unreacted free \(\mathsf{-N H}_{2}\) was \(25.9\%\), which indicated that the reaction was almost sufficient. The reason was that the active site of UiO-66-NH2 was \(25\%\) more than that of PTCDA based on the amount of added reactants. In the FTIR spectra of PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2, the typical \(\mathsf{N{-}H}\) tensile vibrations of amide appeared at \(1250\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), and the \(\mathsf{C{-}N}\) stretching vibration appeared at \(1420\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), which proved that the amino group on UiO-66-NH2 successfully reacted with the acid anhydride bond of PTCDA [37– 39]. From the TEM image in Fig. 3 (f), PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2 had a uniform morphology, and it had lost the typical octahedral structure of UiO-66-NH2.
