## 3.3. Characterization of HPTCDA and HPTCDA-UiO-66-NH2
In Fig. 4 (a), the presence of Na element in the XPS survey spectra of HPTCDA was the evidence that it was hydrolyzed. There was no longer \(\mathsf{C{-}O{-}C}\) in C1s XPS survey spectra of HPTCDA in Fig. 4 (b), and the C(O)-O peak became obvious. And the obvious characteristic peak of \(\mathsf{-COOH}\) appeared at \(1420\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), \(1540\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), \(1590\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1635\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in the FTIR spectra of HPTCDA in Fig. 4 (e). The above proved the complete occurrence of the hydrolysis reaction [40,41]. The simultaneous appearance of Zr, N and Na elements in Fig. 4 (c) proved the successful synthesis of HPTCDA-UiO-66-NH2. Echoing the above, the typical \(\mathsf{N{-}H}\) tensile vibration of amide bond appeared in the FTIR spectra of HPTCDA-UiO-66-NH2 in Fig. 4 (e). It could be known from Fig. 4 (d) that the yield of HPTCDA-UiO-66-NH2 was significantly lower than that of PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2, due to its less \(\mathsf{O{=}C{-}N H}\) than PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2. There were three reasons to explain this situation: Firstly, the carboxyl carbon in the acid anhydride was affected by the carbonyl oxygen and another carboxyl group, which was easily attacked by nucleophiles compared to the carboxyl group; Secondly, when the reaction was finished, they left a carboxylic acid and a hydroxyl group, respectively. And the carboxylic acid was more easily leaving group than that of hydroxyl group; finally, there was no water formed in the reaction of acid anhydride and amino group. It could be found that the water dispersibility of HPTCDA-UiO-66-NH2 nanoparticles was better than that of PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2 from TEM images. The hydrolyzed HPTCDA had very rich carboxyl groups, and these groups adsorbed around MOF nanoparticles due to electrostatic effect, improving its water dispersibility.
## 3.4. Membrane preparation mechanism
The interaction between GO and several other materials could be judged through different peak positions in the UV–Vis full spectrum. In Fig. 5 (b), GO had an obvious characteristic absorption peak at \(230\ \mathrm{nm}\), which was the \(\Pi\)-Π transition absorption of the aromatic ring [23]. However, GO@PTCDA, GO@HPTCDA, GO@PTCDA-UiO-66-NH\(_2\) and GO@HPTCDA-UiO-66-NH\(_2\) generated new peaks around \(230\ \mathrm{nm}\), which was a favorable proof that there
The interaction between GO and several other materials could be judged through different peak positions in the UV–Vis full spectrum. In Fig. 5 (b), GO had an obvious characteristic absorption peak at \(230\ \mathrm{nm}\), which was the \(\Pi\)-Π transition absorption of the aromatic ring [23]. However, GO@PTCDA, GO@HPTCDA, GO@PTCDA-UiO-66-NH\(_2\) and GO@HPTCDA-UiO-66-NH\(_2\) generated new peaks around \(230\ \mathrm{nm}\), which was a favorable proof that there
were the \(\Pi\)-Π conjugate effect between GO and them. The \(\Pi\)-Π transition produced a large \(\Pi\) bond. Due to the close distance between the energy levels of the large \(\Pi\) bond, the electrons were easy to excite, so that the absorption peak shifted to the long wave, causing the characteristic peak to produce a large red shift, which promoted the generation of a new absorption peak [45–47].
In order to explore the value of the interaction between GO and small molecules, molecular dynamics simulation experiments were performed. As shown in Fig. 6, the simulation system was established with GO as the substrate, on whose surface spread small molecules, displaying a 6-layer-sandwich structure, and the simulation results were shown in Table S1 and Table S2. It showed that the interactions between GO and small molecules mainly included \(\Pi\)-Π conjugation and hydrogen bond, and \(\Pi\)-Π conjugation had the dominant advantage. It was worth noting that the \(\Pi\)-Π conjugate energy ratio between GO@PTCDA and GO@HPTCDA was 3.11: 2.66, and the \(\Pi\)-Π conjugate of GO@PTCDA was stronger. This was because the epoxy and 5 saturated C in its anhydride formed an incomplete carbocyclic structure, which made it more advantageous to produce \(\Pi\)-Π conjugate compared to HPTCDA in the ideal simulation environment.
XRD is used to characterize the crystal structure of the material by X-ray diffraction, and the Bragg equation can be used to calculate the lattice size of the internal structure. Although GO is not a typical crystal with crystalline form, it has XRD characteristic peaks, which provides further evidence for the interaction between GO and small molecules. In Fig. 7 (a), GO had a characteristic peak near \(9.5^{\circ}\), which was consistent with the report in the previous literature [48,49]. Table 1 listed the d-spacing of the membranes, and the addition of several substances all made the d-spacing of GO become smaller, which was a predictable result, indicating that there were indeed - interactions between small molecules and GO. However, the d-spacing of \(\mathsf{G O@H P T C D A}\) was smaller than
\(\mathsf{G O@P T C D A}\), which was contrary to the results of MD simulation. In the actual experiment process, the decentralized state of HPTCDA in water was much better than that of PTCDA, which increased the contact chance between HPTCDA and GO, making the interaction between HPTCDA and GO be easier than that of PTCDA. PTCDA tended to self-aggregate in water, reducing its contact chance with GO, and its agglomerates also made the d-spacing of GO larger than HPTCDA. This could also be reflected in Raman spectroscopy. In Fig. 7 (b), GO, \(\mathsf{G O@P T C D A}\), \(\mathsf{G O@H P T C D A}\), \(\mathsf{G O@P T C D A-U i O-66-N H_{2}}\) and \(\mathsf{G O@H P T C D A-U i O-66-N H_{2}}\) all showed typical Raman peaks of carbon atom crystals, corresponding to peaks D and G, respectively. The peak intensity ratio \(\mathrm{(I_{D}/I_{G})}\)
was inversely proportional to the degree of defects [39], and Table 1 listed the relevant data. The data showed that GO@HPTCDA, GO@PTCDA-UiO-66-NH\(_2\), GO@HPTCDA-UiO-66-NH\(_2\) all presented the reduction of internal defects compared to GO, which was mainly attributed to their hydrogen bonding and the \(\Pi\)-Π conjugation effect repairing the defect of the GO sheet. Although there was a large \(\Pi\) bond between PTCDA and GO, PTCDA aggregates made the GO@PTCDA mixture system chaotic and disordered, leading to increased defects. GO@HPTCDA-UiO-66-NH\(_2\) showed smaller defects and d-spacing compared to GO@PTCDA-UiO-66-NH\(_2\), due to more unreacted HPTCDA remained in its system.
Fig. 8 showed the membrane formation mechanism based on the above discussion. As described in the schematic diagram, PTCDA molecule was grafted on the ligand of UiO-66-NH\(_2\). These PTCDA molecules were like ropes that grow on UiO-66-NH\(_2\) connecting with GO through \(\Pi\)-Π conjugation, which was the main force for membrane formation. The abundant oxygen-containing functional groups on the surface of GO formed hydrogen bonds and electrostatic attraction with \(-\mathrm{OH}\) and \(-\mathrm{NH}_2\) in UiO-66-NH\(_2\), which strengthened the stability of PTCDA-UiO-66-NH\(_2\) in the GO sheet. Figure S5 showed the distribution of PTCDA-UiO-66-NH\(_2\) in the GO sheet through the TEM image, and it confirmed that there was indeed an interaction between the two.
3.5. Characterization of different membranes
## 3.5.1. Morphologies of the membranes
Fig. 9 showed the surface morphology and cross-sectional structure of the prepared membranes through SEM and AFM images. The M1 exhibited a typical wrinkle structure belonging to the GO membrane, which was a trace of oxidation, and it was the protruding structure that provided a transport channel for water molecules [2,50]. The regulation of these protrusions was the key to controlling membrane water permeability and rejection. The protrusions on the surface of M2 were the result of self-aggregation of PTCDA, on the contrary, the surface of M3 was very flat. This had been verified in previous discussions: good water dispersibility made HPTCDA and GO produce strong Π-Π conjugate, which caused the wrinkle structure decrease significantly and greatly hinder the passage of water molecules. The addition of large particle size UiO-66-NH\(_2\) greatly improved the undesirability. The M4 and M5 restored the wrinkle structure, appearing the nodular structure that was more conducive to the passage of water. This was because nanoparticles made the GO layer spacing partly expand and produced uniform nodules. This was also confirmed in the AFM and cross-sectional images of the membranes. The membrane thickness of M4 and M5 has increased significantly, while their roughness has become larger compared to M3 in Table 2. The internal disorder of M2 leaded to its maximum roughness. This was also the main reason why its membrane thickness was much larger than M3. The true color confocal microscope can macroscopically characterize the uniformity of the membrane
