## 3.5.2. Performances of the membranes
The water contact angle (WCA) can reflect the hydrophilicity of the membrane surface to a certain extent. As shown in Fig. 10 (a), compared with the GO membrane, WCAs of the other membranes did not change significantly. This indicated that the increase in water permeance did not benefit from the surface hydrophilicity. GO itself was a hydrophilic substance [25,51]. Even though PTCDA was partially hydrolyzed in water, its water dispersibility was far less than GO, which led to the slight increase in WCA of M2. In theory, HPTCDA was very hydrophilic, and the WCA of M3 would greatly reduce. However, in reality, the interaction between HPTCDA and GO shielded some hydrophilic functional groups, while HPTCDA filled the layer spacing of GO. The surface of M3 was dense and extremely smooth, and its water channel was blocked by HPTCDA, which was not conducive to water transmission. Even though the M4 and M5 contained hydrophilic UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), it was mostly embedded between the layer spacing, and its water dispersibility was not as good as GO, so their WCAs was more affected by GO. In Fig. 10 (b), the prepared membranes were all negatively charged, and their negative charge increased with the increase of pH. Compared with M2 and M4, M3 and M5 exhibited a stronger negative charge, which was attributed to the abundant carboxylic acid groups in HPTCDA.
Figure S7 showed the pure water permeance of the prepared membranes, and there was a huge difference of each membrane. Among them, M2 and M3 were the largest and smallest, respec
Figure S7 showed the pure water permeance of the prepared membranes, and there was a huge difference of each membrane. Among them, M2 and M3 were the largest and smallest, respectively. The water permeance of M2 benefited from its biggest membrane defect. The strong hydrophilicity of HPTCDA allowed it to be evenly embedded in the GO layer spacing, which made the typical wrinkle structure on the membrane surface disappear, causing a sharp reduction in the water permeance of M3. The addition of PTCDA-UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) solved the two problems. First, UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) carried a large number of hydroxyl groups [16], so that the water dispersibility of PTCDA-UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was greatly improved compared with PTCDA, which made PTCDA-UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) be uniformly mixed with GO to form a membrane; Second, the large particle size of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) restored the wrinkle structure and re-gave nodular structures that were conducive to water delivery. In Fig. 11, the MB, CR, AZA, EBT, CV and DB9 aqueous solution was used as water pollution models, testing the water treatment capacity of the membranes. It was not difficult to find that M4 showed the best membrane filtration ability, whose removal ability for MB, CR, CV, and DB9 was close to \(100\%\), and its permeance of dye wastewater could be as high as above \(80\ \mathrm{L}\bullet\mathrm{m}^{-2}\bullet\mathrm{h}^{-1}\bullet\mathrm{bar}^{-1}\). For the reason, MB and CR were negatively charged, and they had large molecular weight from Table S3. They could be easily cut off due to molecular sieving and electrostatic repulsion. The rejection of AZA was slightly worse than that of other dyes, even though its molecular weight was almost the same as CR. This was because its water solubility was excellent, so that it penetrated the membrane more easily. The high removal of EBT and DB9 was due to their weak water solubility. The permeability of CV dye wastewater appeared to decrease in the filtration process of M1, M2, M4 and M5. This was because each membrane surface exhibited a strong negative electric potential, but CV presented a positive charge in water from
Table S3. The mutual attraction of opposite charges caused the adsorption fouling layer on the membrane surface, reducing the water permeability of the membrane. Thanks to this, even if the molecular weight of CV was only about 400, it could be successfully rejected by all the prepared membranes. However, compared with other wastewater, the permeance of M3 to CV dye wastewater did not reduce. This meant that the regular arrangement, small layer spacing and smooth surface were effective strategies to avoid the electrostatic adsorption for the GO membrane, and its stability would also be improved, but this would greatly reduce the penetration efficiency of the membrane. The permeance of M5 was lower than that of M4, because more residual HPTCDA blocked
the layer spacing of GO. Table 3 listed the comparison of the separation efficiency of GO@PTCDA-UiO-66-NH\(_2\) membrane with some other membranes, which showed good dye removal ability and considerable water permeability. As comparison, the filter ability to dyes of GO@UiO-66-NH\(_2\) membrane showed in Figure S8. Lacking of the synergy between PTCDA and GO, its removal ability to different dyes decreased, and the positively charged dye was more destructive to its permeance than GO@PTCDA-UiO-66-NH\(_2\) membrane. The contribution of PTCDA to the filtration stability of the composite membrane was confirmed.
In the actual dye wastewater, they are non-neutral, high-salt and with high TOC concentration, causing great pollution to the
environment [1,6,7]. In view of its high TOC concentration, CMC-Na, PDMS, BDC and Span-80 were used as organic pollutants, and the TOC removal performance in wastewater of M4 was studied. The experimental results were shown in Figure S9. The M4 had good removal ability on the four organic substances, and the removal rate of CMC-Na can reach \(98\%\).
Due to the high salt and pH uncertainty of dye wastewater, the water permeance and rejection of M4 at different salt concentrations and pH were studied, respectively. \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) was used as the salt model, and HCl and NaOH were used to adjust the acidity and alkalinity of the wastewater model, respectively. As shown in Fig. 12 (a, b), when the salt concentration increased to \(1\ \mathrm{w/v}\ \%\) , the permeance of M4 increased significantly, and the rejection decreased slightly. With the continuous increase of the salt concentration, the permeance gradually decreased, and the rejection returned to the original level. Even if the salt concentration had increased to \(9\ \mathrm{w/v}\ \%\) , the permeance of M4 had not been significantly attenuated, which was even higher than that in a salt-free environment. This indicated that the membrane could maintain stable permeance and rejection in a high-salt environment. The influence of salt concentration on the dye separation could be explained by steric and electrostatic effects. When the salt concentration was low, the dye molecules and salt ions combined to make the dispersion be more uniform [59], and it was easier to penetrate the membrane for dye, so that the rejection was reduced. The con
centration polarization and membrane fouling also reduced, and the permeance increased. When the salt concentration increased to high salt \((>1\ \mathrm{w/v}\ \%)\), the dye viscosity and membrane resistance also improved, which caused dye molecules to agglomerate, increasing rejection. In addition, dyes and salts accumulated on the membrane surface to form concentration polarization and increase osmotic pressure, thereby reducing permeance [60,61]. In Fig. 13 (a, b), the M4 exhibited good removal rate to the six dyes at the pH range of 2 to 8, which showed that the separation performance of the membrane was stable under a relatively large pH span. When the pH was 10, the rejection of EBT, CV, and DB9 decreased significantly, which was because the alkali tolerance of \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{CCNH}_{2}\) decreased at high pH [30,62]. However, as the pH gradually decreased, the permeance of M4 also decreased. The reason was the hydrophilic groups \(-\mathrm{OH}\) and \(-\mathrm{COOH}\) on the surface of GO were shielded by a large amount of \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\)in an acidic environment, which could be evidenced by the weak electronegativity on the membrane surface at low pH in Fig. 10 (b).
Screening is the most important rejection mechanism for nanofiltration membrane [63]. The d-spacing is an important reference for GO membrane. However, it was obvious that the d-spacing of M4 was larger than some of dye molecules. It has been given an explanation in Fig. 14: GO is composed of sheets with a certain curvature and not a perfect 2D structure, and its d-spacing will also change with physical filling and chemical connection. Although
PTCDA-UiO-66-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) greatly expanded the layer spacing of GO, the "actual distance" between \(\mathrm{PTCDA-UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) and the sheets became closer, and these "actual distances" were the direct physical barriers that blocked the dye molecules. The d-spacing was the average distance between two adjacent layers, and the smaller d-spacing of M4 was the direct evidence that the "actual distance" had become closer. Therefore, the screening mechanism was still the most important in the filtration process of M4. It could not achieve sufficient rejection of some small-size dyes relying solely on size screening. In order to simulate other mechanisms of dye removal, three different electronegative dyes of CV, CR, DB9 were used as wastewater pollutants in the adsorption experiment in Fig. 15 (a, b). In the initial stage of adsorption, CV molecules were quickly attracted to the membrane surface. After a period of time, the adsorption of CR by the membrane surpassed CV. This was very easy to explain. The mutual attraction of positive and negative charges made the membrane and CV have the fastest adsorption behavior. Although CR carried the negative charge, its sulfonic acid groups and aromatic hydrocarbons could easily occur II-II stacking and hydrogen bonding with GO and \(\mathrm{PTCDA-UiO-66-NH}_{2}\), so that the adsorption capacity to CR gradually exceed CV. DB9 itself was not charged, and its adsorption was mostly hydrogen bonds, van der Waals forces and other weaker intermolecular forces. Therefore, its adsorption behavior was not obvious. In Fig. 15 (c), the adsorption behavior of molecules was simulated, which confirmed the above statement. At first, the electrostatic energy growth rate of CV surpassed CR. As time passed, CV was gradually surpassed by CR. Moreover, regardless of electrostatic energy and potential energy, the overall absorption of CR by the membrane was greater than CV. And it showed the worst adsorption behavior for DB9. It was also not difficult to find from the adsorption curve that M4 took a longer time to reach adsorption saturation compared to M1, and its adsorption capacity to the three dyes was greater than that of M1. The reason was \(\mathrm{PTCDA-UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) was a good adsorbent in the membrane structure. The PTCDA was multiple aromatic rings structure forming II-II stacking with dye molecules, and \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) was a porous material with high specific surface [64,65]. In addition, for the adsorbed membranes, the color of M4 was slightly darker than that of M1, which also confirmed the adsorption contribution of \(\mathrm{PTCDA-UiO-66-NH}_{2}\).
Based on the above membrane screening and adsorption behavior discussion, we had constructed a clear membrane filtration process. In Fig. 16, the membrane filtration was mainly through the vertical filtration of the GO and the lateral adsorption of PTCDA-UiO-66-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\). Most of the dye molecules were filtered out vertically, and a small part of the dyes were adsorbed by \(\mathrm{PTCDA-UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) in the horizontal direction. In addition to the most important
screening filtration, the vertical filtration was also attributed to the forces of II-II stacking, hydrogen bonding, electrostatic effects, etc. PTCDA-UiO-66-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) carried some \(-\mathrm{OH}\) and \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), and the PTCDA and terephthalic acid in it also had benzene rings, so it could form the above forces with the dye molecules, realizing the complete removal of the dyes. This was the main principle of its horizontal adsorption of dyes.
## 3.5.3. Stability of the membrane
The swelling experiment and ultrasonic experiment were carried out to explore the contribution of PTCDA to membrane structure stability. In Figure S10, soaked for \(60\;\mathrm{h}\), the swelling rates of the supporting membrane, M1 and M4 were \(144.87\%\), \(129.33\%\) and \(111.69\%\), respectively. However, the swelling rate of \(\mathsf{G O@U i O-66-N H}_{2}\) membrane has reached \(208.47\%\) after soaked for \(45\,\mathrm{h}\) in Figure S11. And its self-assembled membrane has been completely broken after ultrasonic treatment for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\). In Figure S12, M1 was damaged after \(40\ \mathrm{min}\) ultrasonic treatment, and M4 did not experience obvious damage during the entire experiment. These confirmed that the insertion of \(\mathsf{U i O-66-N H}_{2}\) would destroy the original structural stability of GO due to the few interaction forces between them. Joining of PTCDA promoted the compatibility of PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2 and GO sheets because of the conjugation effect between PTCDA and GO, and the \(\mathsf{G O@P T C D A-U i O-66-N H}_{2}\) complex became more compact and not easy to damage. In addition, this reduced the membrane filtration loss caused by membrane swelling and prolonged the service life of the membrane.
Considering economy, the recovery performance of the membrane was very important. As shown in Fig. 17, CR, CV and DB9 were used as simulated wastewater pollutants to carry out cycling experiments on M4. Experiments showed that M4 maintained close to \(100\%\) removal rate for the three dyes within 6 cycles, while the permeability was slightly reduced due to the adsorption pollution of dyes on the surface of M4. Because of the membrane swelling caused by multiple cycles of cleaning, the removal rate of M4 decreased in the later stage of the cycle experiment. Especially for uncharged DB9, it rarely had adsorption behavior with M4, so the membrane swelling caused its rejection to continue to decrease. Similarly, it was not easy to adhere to the surface of M4, and its permeability was significantly improved. For the charged dyes of
CR and CV, the interaction between them with M4 filled up the structural relaxation caused by membrane swelling. After 10 cycles, the rejections of CR and CV were always above \(93\%\) and \(95\%\), respectively. Overall, M4 had a certain degree of recyclability in filtering dye wastewater. In addition, the surface morphologies of M4 before and after the adsorption and cycle experiment were characterized by SEM (Figure S13). The characterization results showed that the membrane material after the adsorption and cycle test maintained its original form and structure, which proved its good structural stability.
## 4. Conclusion
In short, a new type \(\mathsf{G O@P T C D A-U i O-66-N H}_{2}\) membrane was prepared by vacuum filtration, and UiO-66-NH2 modified by PTCDA greatly enhanced the binding force of MOFs and GO sheets. Experiments showed that the strong II-II conjugation between GO and PTCDA fixed PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2 firmly in the GO sheets, while the hydrogen bonding were assisted. The success preparation and surface morphology of the membranes were showed at SEM and AFM image. The results proved that PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2 restored the GO water delivery channel, and created a new nodular structure that was more conducive to water delivery. Due to the insertion of PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2, the actual filter channel became narrower, which was conducive to the removal of dyes. In addition, PTCDA-UiO-66-NH2 brought lateral secondary adsorption to the dyes entering the membrane, making the entire membrane form a unique vertical screening-horizontal adsorption filtration structure, further improving the separation performance. The permeability of \(\mathsf{G O@P T C D A-U i O-66-N H}_{2}\) membrane could reach 83.47 \(\mathsf{L}\bullet\mathsf{m}^{-2}\bullet\mathsf{h}^{-1}\bullet\mathsf{b}\mathsf{a}\mathsf{r}^{-1}\), and its removal ability of MB, CR, CV and DB9 was close to \(100\%\). In the pH range of 2–8, its dye removal ability remained unchanged. It could tolerate \(9\mathsf{w}/\mathsf{v}\%\) salt content, and
the membrane permeability and rejection were not attenuated. Moreover, after 6 cyclic experiments, the dye removal remained at the original level. This was the first time that the small molecule PTCDA had been used to enhance the compatibility of MOFs and GO, and this mutual coordination between GO and PTCDA-UiO\(66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) overcome the trade-off effect, bringing a good applied prospect for GO membrane.
